3:## What changed
13:| src/lsp.rs | +175 −26 | resumed run: navigate object methods. `to can name(...)` declarations are tracked per object, a member followed by `(` resolves to the method while a bare member resolves to the field, and `has`/method declarations get distinct bindings so `has name` and `to can name()` no longer collapse into one entry. Review fixes: a plain-name call prefers a same-scope function over a same-named field. Round-2 fixes: a member or type query answers only its own binding, goto on a type name no longer falls through to a builtin, and an object field is a member read through `this`/the object name rather than a lexical name. |
15:| src/lsp.rs | +36 −14 | resumed run: model `if`/`unless` branch bodies as their own scope, as the analyzer does. A `set` inside the body writes through to an enclosing binding (or the program scope when the name is new), so it survives `end`, while a function, object or import declared there does not leak. Round-3 review fixes: a `test` body gets its own scope because the harness parses each body separately and runs it in an isolated VM, a `constant` always binds in the program scope (the test body's scope inside a `test`) instead of reusing an enclosing `set`, and the catch scope closes at `finally` so the cleanup body is analyzed in the enclosing scope without the catch binder. |
19:| src/lsp.rs | +66 −2 | round-2 review: a `constant` declares its own program-scope binding instead of reusing an earlier `set` of the same spelling, and the deferred pass keeps a name already resolved in source order while still repairing the field/method distinction. |
25:| src/lsp.rs | +47 −15 | round-2 review: a builtin use written before a later first `set` or function stays unbound so the builtin fallback answers, while a preceding declaration or a `constant`/`import` still claims the name; object fields no longer enter lexical scopes, matching the analyzer, and member accesses re-resolve after both member tables are complete so `this.name` still prefers a field declared after its method. |
26:| tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +116 −15 | round-2 review tests: a builtin before a later `set abs` keeps the builtin definition and reference set while the variable keeps its own; a bare field read inside a method body has no binding while `this.name` resolves; a position past a line with a builtin and a later `set` is a clean error from all three capabilities; and the field-declared-after-its-method fixture now reads `this.name` and asserts the field's references include that member read. |
33:| src/lsp.rs | +18 −4 | round-2 review fixes: a replacing `set` also points the current block scope at its new variable, so a function declared and then shadowed in the same branch body cannot answer from the stale block entry; and a called name only reaches a function declared before the use, so a call after a shadowing `set` does not jump forward to a later `to`. |
34:| tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +85 −0 | two round-2 review tests: a `set` shadows a function declared in the same `if` body while the earlier call keeps the function's references, and a call between a shadowing `set` and a later `to` does not reach the later function. |
39:| src/lsp.rs | +102 −2 | round-2 review: the analysis records each `module … end` body's top-level `to`, `set` and `constant` names and its last `export`, keyed by the module binding, and filters them into a declared-module member table when the module closes. `member_completions` consults that table before `MODULE_MEMBERS`, so a program-declared module (and an import of it) offers what it publishes even when its name is also a stdlib module's. The flat alias table is now consulted only for a receiver that has an in-scope `Import` binding, so an alias written inside a function or branch cannot answer after its `end`. |
40:| tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +106 −0 | four round-2 review tests: `module text … end` offers its exported members rather than the stdlib `text` members; an explicit `export` filters a declared module's names; an import alias of a declared module offers its members; and an import alias used after its function or branch block offers nothing. |
43:| src/lsp.rs | +30 −9 | final round-1 review fixes: the published-function table is keyed by the module binding rather than its name, so a redeclaration or a same-named module in another block cannot answer for the first module's calls, and a module declaration gives a redeclaration its own binding; a module-top-level `set` removes the same-named function entry because it replaced the member value (the round-3 review corrected the `constant` half: a module `constant` binds a program global and leaves the function published); and a bare `Module.name` read resolves through the same table as a call, matching the runtime's exposed function value. |
45:| src/lsp.rs | +42 −10 | round-2 review fixes: a `set` inside a nested block removes the replaced module function by finding the innermost runtime frame's module rather than looking only at the top frame (the round-3 review narrowed this from `set` or `constant` to `set`, because a constant is a global), and an `import` records the module binding it named at its own site, so an alias written before a later same-named module keeps the earlier module's functions and completed members. |
50:| tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +99 −0 | three round-2 review tests: a nested branch's `set` drops the stale module function (the `constant` half was corrected in round 3); an alias between two same-named modules resolves and completes from the module its import named, not the later declaration. |
58:| src/lsp.rs | +236 −34 | round-2 review: brace pairing is a single stack pass over the decoded literal, candidates are tested from the inside out under a bounded work budget, a valid pair skips the span it replaces exactly as the parser does, and literals whose decoded text cannot contain the queried name are skipped. All substitutions in one literal are re-analyzed together instead of analyzing the whole file once per pair; that analysis is capped per query and reused for every use in the literal. Nested text literals inside an interpolated expression are walked through the resulting view, so their substitutions still contribute references. |
59:| tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +90 −0 | round-2 review tests: interpolation references now assert `end_line`/`end_column` for the declaration, a use followed by text, a use that fills the expression, and an escaped-brace use; a builtin inside `{abs(-3)}` is queried with `find_references` and maps back to its source span. |
62:| src/lsp.rs | +20 −8 | round-3 review: the owning module for a published value is the innermost runtime frame's module, so a replacing `set` inside an `if`/loop in a module body is recorded exactly as the function-removal walk already found it; and a `constant` is no longer treated as a module value at all. The VM binds a module `constant` to a program global, so it is neither published by the namespace nor a replacement for a same-named module function. |
63:| tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs | +50 −20 | round-3 review tests: a `set` written in a nested branch resolves a later bare `M.f` and shares its references; a module `constant` does not replace the published function at the body's top level or inside a branch; and the module-value fixture now asserts a `constant` member answers `None` with no references. |
64:| tests/module_system_test.rs | +24 −0 | round-3 golden test: a module's `set` is a published member while its `constant` is a program global the module does not publish, pinned against the runtime's own member table. |
